 ## PI-INTERACTION-064: Crashes report themselves
 
 When a client crashes or the runtime reports a fault, Flywheel sends a bug report to the Flywheel maintainers on its own, and the crash screen or fault notice says that it sent one and shows a short report id the person can quote. A report holds the error, the app version and build, the platform, the surface, and the recent diagnostic records of the client, the account service, and the project executor; it holds no conversation or file content. The person can also send a report with an optional note at any time with the report command. When a report cannot be sent, the screen says so and offers to copy the report.
+
+## PI-INTERACTION-065: Unused chats can delete themselves
+
+Settings offers automatic deletion of unused chats, off by default. When a person turns it on with a whole number of days, Flywheel deletes each chat that no one has used and that has done no work for that many days, with the workflows and child sessions its deletion removes, the same as a person's delete. A chat that is open in a tab, working, or owning running work is never deleted this way. Flywheel deletes in a project when the project opens, once a day while it stays open, and when the setting changes.
+
+## PI-INTERACTION-066: An activity turns red only for a real stall
+
+In the terminal, the status line shows how long the current activity has gone without progress from the model. Each activity starts in its neutral color when it appears and turns red only after time spent in it with no new output or activity change. A wait on subagents or a reply, compaction, a notice, or a lost connection is not a stall: while one shows, no stall time builds up, and the activity that follows starts neutral.
+
+## PI-INTERACTION-067: Settings save as they change
+
+On every client, a change to a setting is saved as soon as the person makes it. No settings surface has a Save key or a Save button. A choice, such as a checkbox, a switch, or a highlighted theme, saves when the person changes it. A model and its effort are one choice: the person adjusts both, and confirming the model saves them together. A typed value, such as a number or a name, saves when the person finishes it: on Enter, on leaving the field, on Escape, or when the surface closes. A typed value that is not valid is not saved, and the field says why. Each control shows the saved value: while a change is being saved the control says so, and a change that cannot be saved returns the control to the saved value with a notice that says why. Closing or leaving a settings surface never discards a change. Entering an API key is a connection step and is submitted explicitly. A bulk change that turns off every module asks for confirmation first.
